<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    Graphics/k_sprites.xml is a custom-named sprite bank, loaded explicitly
    by KirbyHelperMechanicsModule.LoadContent via SpriteBank.LoadSpriteBank;
    it must NOT be named Graphics/Sprites.xml. DZ's own
    Mods/DZ/Graphics/Sprites.xml already defines an element also named
    "kirby_player_ext" (with a different, "kirby_"-prefixed animation
    scheme: start="kirby_idle", ids like kirby_idle/kirby_walk/kirby_run
    instead of this mod's vanilla-mirrored idle/walk/runFast). Everest
    auto-merges every mod's Graphics/Sprites.xml into one shared bank, and
    DZ's copy gets processed after this mod's, so if this file were also
    named Sprites.xml, DZ's "kirby_idle"-based kirby_player_ext would
    silently win and KirbyPlayerController's RenderKirbyOverlay (which
    calls Sprite.Play with vanilla's own CurrentAnimationID, e.g. "idle")
    would find no matching animation: the Kirby overlay would render but
    never animate. Loading this file explicitly from LoadContent (which
    runs after every mod's Load(), i.e. after Everest's initial auto-merge)
    guarantees this mod's kirby_player_ext always overwrites whatever DZ's
    auto-merge left behind, regardless of mod load order.

    The atlas folders below live under characters/KHM/, cutscenes/KHM/, and
    objects/KHM/, NOT characters/DZ/ etc, for the same reason: DZ ships its
    own separately-authored Kirby skin at characters/DZ/kirby/ (and
    characters/DZ/kirby_Playback/, cutscenes/DZ/kirby_payphone/,
    objects/DZ/kirby/{booster,lookout}/). Everest's asset resolution is
    per-file, not per-mod-per-folder: with both mods installed, any PNG
    that exists at the same relative path in both mods' Graphics/Atlases/
    trees resolves to whichever mod's copy loads last (DZ, in practice),
    silently discarding this mod's copy for that one frame, while frames
    this mod has that DZ doesn't (e.g. the extended jumpFast/fall frames,
    or the extra/levelUp/roll/land additions) are unaffected, since there's
    nothing to lose to. The net effect used to be individual animations
    rendering as an inconsistent mix of DZ's art and this mod's art, not
    just a clean "one mod wins" swap. Using the KHM/ namespace instead of
    DZ/ makes every one of this mod's asset paths unique, so nothing here
    can ever collide with DZ's (or any other mod's) content again.
    Consolidates the Kirby-reskinned atlas folders used by Kirby Helper
    Mechanics' KirbyPlayerController:

      characters/KHM/kirby            -> kirby_player_ext (read by KirbyPlayerController.Added)
      characters/KHM/kirby_Playback   -> kirby_playback
      cutscenes/KHM/kirby_payphone    -> kirby_payphone
      objects/KHM/kirby/booster       -> kirby_booster / kirby_boosterRed
      objects/KHM/kirby/lookout       -> kirby_lookout

    kirby_playback/kirby_payphone/kirby_booster/kirby_lookout are staged for
    future entities (dash-route ghost, cutscene NPC, reskinned booster/lookout)
    that don't exist in Kirby Helper Mechanics yet; only kirby_player_ext is
    currently read by code (KirbyPlayerController.Added: GFX.SpriteBank.Create("kirby_player_ext")).

    kirby_player_ext's core movement/state block below is vanilla Celeste's
    own "player" entry (Content/Graphics/Sprites.xml), copied id-for-id,
    frame-slice-for-frame-slice, hair-metadata-for-hair-metadata; only the
    atlas `path` on the root element changed, from characters/player/ to
    characters/KHM/kirby/, since this atlas's frame folders were built to the
    same layout (idle/idleA/idleB/idleC, runSlow/runFast/runStumble/runWind,
    jumpSlow/jumpFast/climb/dreamDash/swim/sleep/wakeUp/etc all match vanilla's
    own frame counts almost exactly). This mirrors the "component riding the
    real Player" rewrite (see KirbyPlayerHooks.cs): KirbyPlayerController.
    RenderKirbyOverlay() copies the REAL vanilla Player.Sprite's
    CurrentAnimationID onto this bank's Sprite every frame, so whatever
    vanilla's own UpdateSprite decides (idle/run/climb/dash/duck/...) "just
    works" as long as the id strings match, exactly the same idea as
    Picoline's player_atlas.png reskinning vanilla Player without changing
    vanilla's own animation-selection logic. The hair Metadata block is inert
    for Kirby (KirbyPlayerController hides the real Player's Hair component
    entirely) but kept for parity/future use.

    Appended after the vanilla-mirrored block: a small set of Kirby-only
    ability ids (kirby_inhale_*/kirby_hover/kirby_float/kirby_spit/
    kirby_damage/kirby_transform_*/kirby_*_death/kirby_angry/kirby_levelUp)
    that have no vanilla equivalent; KirbyPlayerController's Float/Inhale/
    StarSpit state callbacks call KirbySprite.Play(...) on those directly,
    bypassing the per-frame vanilla-id mirroring for the duration of those
    states.

    kirby_sweat is a separate companion-overlay entry (own root element, not
    part of kirby_player_ext) for the "sweat/" atlas folder, nervous/danger
    idle-fidget frames meant to be drawn as a second Sprite layered over
    kirby_player_ext, the same pattern as a face/eye overlay riding a body
    sprite. Nothing in Kirby Helper Mechanics reads it yet; staged like
    kirby_playback/kirby_payphone/kirby_booster/kirby_lookout above.

    Combat animations (extra frames under characters/KHM/kirby/combat/:
    backflip_/grab_enemy_/groundpound_/punchA_/punchB_/slide_/spin_/
    super_punch*) are deliberately NOT wired up as ids here; only the
    death-sequence frames from that same folder are. See the "Combat
    Animations - Not Added (as requested)" note inline below.

    Dash-count color grading LUTs (not part of the SpriteBank schema below;
    documented here only because this is the index of Kirby-reskin asset
    folders): Graphics/ColorGrading/KHM/kirby/{name}.png, one loose top-level
    PNG per file, not packed into an atlas. This mirrors DZ's own top-level
    Graphics/ColorGrading/ convention (loaded via Everest.Content.TryGet on
    "Graphics/ColorGrading/{lutTextureName}", see DZ's
    Source/Graphics/Effects/ColorGradeEffect.cs) rather than DZ's separate,
    unrelated per-character-skin convention nested inside the sprite atlas
    itself (characters/DZ/<skin>/ColorGrading/DashN.png, e.g. Ralsei's).

      Graphics/ColorGrading/KHM/kirby/dash0.png      -> reference key "KHM/kirby/dash0"
      Graphics/ColorGrading/KHM/kirby/dash0safe.png  -> reference key "KHM/kirby/dash0safe"
      Graphics/ColorGrading/KHM/kirby/dash2.png      -> reference key "KHM/kirby/dash2"
      Graphics/ColorGrading/KHM/kirby/dash3.png      -> reference key "KHM/kirby/dash3"
      Graphics/ColorGrading/KHM/kirby/dash4.png      -> reference key "KHM/kirby/dash4"
      Graphics/ColorGrading/KHM/kirby/none.png       -> reference key "KHM/kirby/none"

    The reference key is the path under Graphics/ColorGrading/ without the
    .png extension; whatever eventually applies these (a ColorGradeEffect-
    style Backdrop, or code reading Player.Dashes directly) would pass that
    key string, e.g. "KHM/kirby/dash2" for Dashes == 2. Like
    kirby_playback/kirby_payphone/kirby_booster/kirby_lookout/kirby_sweat
    above, nothing in Kirby Helper Mechanics reads these yet; KHM has no
    ColorGradeEffect-equivalent Backdrop ported from DZ, so they're currently
    orphaned assets.
-->
<Sprites>

    <kirby_player_ext path="characters/KHM/kirby/" start="idle">
      <Origin x="16" y="32" />

      <Loop id="idle_carry" path="idle_carry" delay="0.1" />
      <Anim id="runSlow_carry" path="run_carry" delay="0.07" />
      <Loop id="jumpSlow_carry" path="jump_carry" delay="0.1" frames="0,1" />
      <Anim id="fallSlow_carry" path="jump_carry" delay="0.1" frames="2,3" />
      <Anim id="pickUp" path="pickup" delay="0.06" />
      <Anim id="throw" path="throw" delay="0.06" goto="idle" />

      <Anim id="idle" path="idle" delay="0.1" goto="idle" />

      <Anim id="idleA" path="idleA" delay="0.12" goto="idle" />
      <Anim id="idleB" path="idleB" delay="0.12" goto="idle" />
      <Anim id="idleC" path="idleC" delay="0.05" goto="idle" />

      <Anim id="lookUp" path="lookUp" delay="0.1" frames="2-7" />
      <Loop id="walk" path="walk" delay="0.06" />
      <Loop id="push" path="push" delay="0.1" />
      <Anim id="runSlow" path="runSlow" delay="0.07" goto="runFast" />
      <Loop id="runFast" path="runFast" delay="0.05" />
      <Anim id="runStumble" path="runStumble" delay="0.05" frames="10-11,0-11" goto="runFast" />
      <Loop id="runWind" path="run_wind" delay="0.095" frames="0-11" />
      <Loop id="dash" path="dash" delay="0.09" />
      <Anim id="dreamDashIn" path="dreamDash" frames="0-3" delay="0.04" goto="dreamDashLoop" />
      <Loop id="dreamDashLoop" path="dreamDash" frames="4-16" delay="0.03" />
      <Anim id="dreamDashOut" path="dreamDash" frames="17-20" delay="0.04" />
      <Loop id="slide" path="slide" delay="0.03" />
      <Loop id="jumpSlow" path="jumpSlow" delay="0.1" frames="0,1" />
      <Loop id="jumpFast" path="jumpFast" delay="0.1" frames="0,1" />
      <!-- jumpFast has 15 frames (0-14), not vanilla's 4; frames 2-14 were
           previously unused. This hangtime sequence (a held near-apex pose,
           a long hold on the falling pose, then a quick landing flourish)
           uses the full set for both fallSlow and fallFast. -->
      <Anim id="fallSlow" path="jumpFast" delay="0.060" frames="2,2,2,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,12,12,13,13,14,14" />
      <Anim id="fallFast" path="jumpFast" delay="0.060" frames="2,2,2,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,11,12,12,13,13,14,14" />
      <Loop id="tired" path="tired" delay="0.18" />
      <Anim id="tiredStill" path="tired" frames="0" />
      <Loop id="wallslide" path="climb" frames="0" />
      <Anim id="climbLookBackStart" path="climb" delay="0.08" frames="6,7,8" goto="climbLookBack" />
      <Loop id="climbLookBack" path="climb" frames="8" />
      <Loop id="climbup" path="climb" delay="0.04" frames="0-5" />
      <Anim id="climbPush" path="climb" delay="0.04" frames="0,9-11" />
      <Anim id="climbPull" path="climb" delay="0.04" frames="0,12-14" />
      <Loop id="duck" path="duck" delay="0" frames="0" />
      <Anim id="fallPose" path="fallPose" delay="0.1" frames="0-10" goto="idle" />
      <Loop id="edge" path="edge" delay="0.25" frames="0-13" />
      <Loop id="edgeBack" path="edge_back" delay="0.25" />
      <Anim id="faint" path="faint" delay="0.1" frames="0-10" goto="fainted" />
      <Loop id="fainted" path="faint" delay="0.1" frames="10" />
      <Anim id="flip" path="flip" delay="0.04" frames="0-7" goto="runFast" />
      <Loop id="skid" path="flip" delay="0.04" frames="8" />
      <Loop id="dangling" path="dangling" delay="0.11" frames="0-9" />
      <Anim id="deadside" path="death_h" delay="0.02" frames="0-14" />
      <Anim id="deadup" path="death_h" delay="0.02" frames="0-14" />
      <Anim id="deaddown" path="death_h" delay="0.02" frames="0-14" />

      <Loop id="swimIdle" path="swim" frames="0-5" delay="0.08" />
      <Loop id="swimUp" path="swim" frames="6-11" delay="0.08" />
      <Loop id="swimDown" path="swim" frames="12-17" delay="0.08" />

      <Anim id="startStarFly" path="startStarFly" delay="0.08" goto="starFly" />
      <Loop id="starFly" path="starFly" delay="0.08" />
      <Loop id="bubble" path="bubble" delay="0.08" />

      <Anim id="fall" path="fall" delay="0.06" frames="0-7" />
      <Loop id="bigFall" path="bigfall" frames="0-4" delay="0.06" />
      <Anim id="bigFallRecover" path="bigfall" frames="5*5,6*4,7*3,8,8,9,9,10,10,10" delay="0.08" goto="swimIdle" />

      <Anim id="sleep" path="sleep" delay="0.1" frames="0-10,10*5,11-23" />
      <Anim id="bagdown" path="sleep" delay="0.1" frames="0-10" />
      <Anim id="asleep" path="wakeUp/" frames="0" />
      <Anim id="wakeUp" path="wakeUp/" delay=".1" frames="0-4,5*10,6-14" />
      <Anim id="halfWakeUp" path="halfWakeUp" delay=".1" />

      <Loop id="spin" path="spin" delay="0.1" />
      <Loop id="shaking" path="shaking" delay="0.1" />
      <Loop id="hug" path="hug" delay="0.08" />

      <Anim id="starMorph" path="starMorph" delay="0.06" frames="0-16" goto="starMorphIdle" />
      <Loop id="starMorphIdle" path="starMorph" frames="17" delay="0.06" />

      <Loop id="carryTheoWalk" path="walk_carry_theo" frames="0-11" delay="0.06" />
      <Anim id="carryTheoCollapse" path="walk_carry_theo" frames="12-18" delay="0.06" />

      <Anim id="tentacle_grab" path="tentacle/grab" delay="0.06" frames="0-14" goto="tentacle_grabbed" />
      <Loop id="tentacle_grabbed" path="tentacle/grab" delay="0.1" frames="15-23" />
      <Loop id="tentacle_pull" path="tentacle/grab" frames="24" delay="0.1" />
      <Loop id="tentacle_dangling" path="tentacle/grab" frames="25" delay="0.1" />

      <Anim id="sitDown" path="sitDown" delay="0.1" />

      <Loop id="launch" path="launch" delay="0.06" frames="0-7" />
      <Anim id="launchRecover" path="launchRecover" delay="0.06" />

      <!-- Trimmed to just the "carry" entries: those directly affect where a
           carried object (e.g. Theo) is drawn relative to Kirby and are
           functionally live. The rest of vanilla's hair-position Frames data
           is dropped rather than kept-but-stale, since it's fully inert for
           Kirby: KirbyPlayerController hides the real Player's Hair
           component entirely (see the file header comment). -->
      <Metadata>
        <Frames path="idle_carry" hair="x" carry="-1,-1,-1,0,0,0,0,0,-1" />
        <Frames path="jump_carry" hair="x" carry="-3,-3,-1,-1" />
        <Frames path="run_carry" hair="x" carry="-1,0,0,0,-3,-2,-1,0,0,0,-3,-1" />
      </Metadata>

      <!-- Kirby-only abilities (no vanilla equivalent); KirbyPlayerController
           plays these directly during StKirbyFloat/StKirbyInhale/StKirbyStarSpit,
           bypassing the per-frame vanilla-id mirroring for those states. -->
      <Anim id="kirby_inhale_start" path="inhalebegin" delay="0.06" goto="kirby_inhale_loop" />
      <Loop id="kirby_inhale_loop" path="inhaleloop" delay="0.08" />
      <Anim id="kirby_inhale_end" path="inhaleend" delay="0.06" goto="idle" />
      <Loop id="kirby_hover" path="hover" delay="0.12" frames="0-1" />
      <Loop id="kirby_float" path="float" delay="0.12" frames="0-1" />
      <Anim id="kirby_spit" path="spit" delay="0.05" />
      <Anim id="kirby_damage" path="hurt" delay="0.08" goto="idle" />

      <!-- Transformation Animations. NOTE: path is "kirby_transform_in"/
           "kirby_transform_out", NOT the bare "transform_in"/"transform_out"
           folders: the bare "transform_in" set is missing its first two
           frames (starts at transform_in02.png), an incomplete leftover
           export; "kirby_transform_in00-19.png" is the complete 20-frame
           sequence. "kirby_transform_out" and bare "transform_out" are both
           complete/identical, but kept the "kirby_"-prefixed one for
           consistency with transform_in. -->
      <Anim id="kirby_transform_in" path="kirby_transform_in" delay="0.06" goto="idle" />
      <Anim id="kirby_transform_out" path="kirby_transform_out" delay="0.06" />

      <!-- Reactions: "extra/" and "levelUp/" atlas folders, previously unreferenced -->
      <Anim id="kirby_angry" path="extra/angry" delay="0.08" frames="0-5" goto="idle" />
      <Anim id="kirby_levelUp" path="levelUp/levelUp" delay="0.05" frames="0-26" goto="idle" />

      <!-- Combat Animations - Not Added (as requested); Mouthful/Swallow below
           use real dedicated art (idlemouthful/, walkmouthful/, exhale/), not
           placeholders; only Range and Copy still borrow another id's frames
           since no dedicated "range"/"copy" art exists yet. -->
      <Anim id="kirby_range" path="spit" delay="0.05" goto="idle" />
      <Loop id="kirby_mouthful" path="idlemouthful" delay="0.08" />
      <Loop id="kirby_walkmouthful" path="walkmouthful" delay="0.06" />
      <Anim id="kirby_swallow" path="exhale" delay="0.08" goto="idle" />
      <Anim id="kirby_copy" path="kirby_transform_in" delay="0.06" goto="idle" />

      <!-- Staged for a future roll-attack/landing-impact ability; no
           KirbyPlayerController state calls these yet (same "staged" pattern
           as kirby_booster/kirby_lookout below), but the atlas has dedicated
           roll/, rollGetup/, rollSit.png, and land/ art already. Roll chains
           into a getup recovery that settles into a held "downed"/resting
           pose (rollSit) rather than snapping straight back to idle; a future
           controller integration decides how/when that resolves back to
           idle (timer vs. input). -->
      <Anim id="kirby_roll" path="roll" delay="0.05" />
      <Anim id="kirby_rollGetup" path="rollGetup" delay="0.1" goto="kirby_rollSit" />
      <Loop id="kirby_rollSit" path="rollSit" delay="0.8" />
      <Anim id="kirby_land" path="land" delay="0.05" goto="idle" />

      <!-- Death Animations -->
      <Anim id="kirby_pre_death" path="combat/pre_death_" delay="0.08" goto="kirby_mid_death" />
      <Anim id="kirby_mid_death" path="combat/mid_death_" delay="0.08" goto="kirby_post_death" />
      <Anim id="kirby_post_death" path="combat/post_death_" delay="0.08" />
      <Anim id="kirby_death" path="combat/death_" delay="0.08" />
    </kirby_player_ext>

